  journal   = "Sci. Total Environ.",
  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  958,
  number    =  177664,
  pages     =  177664,
  abstract  = "Freshwater mussels are experiencing severe population declines,
               affecting their critical role in freshwater ecosystems. A
               thorough assessment of threats posed by various stressors is
               needed; however, the large number of species to be considered and
               significant data gaps, especially for listed species, hinder the
               process. We combined a traits-based approach to represent
               multiple species grouped into three life-history categories -
               Equilibrium, Opportunistic, and Periodic - with the Dynamic
               Energy Budget modeling principles to capture the physiological
               mechanisms driving individual-level responses. We used the DEB
               model to simulate individual life cycles and explore
               relationships between underlying energetics and emerging
               individual traits of 47 freshwater mussel species and the common
               toxicity test surrogate, the Eastern oyster (Crassostrea
               virginica), under control and stressed conditions. Stress was
               introduced via physiological modes of action related to four key
               metabolic pathways: energy assimilation, maintenance, growth, and
               reproduction. We recorded maximum length, age at maturity, and
               fecundity and compared these endpoints and their stress-induced
               changes among life-history categories. The life-history
               differences among freshwater mussels directly emerged from
               underlying energetics, with high assimilation and maintenance
               supporting opportunistic traits. Stress imposed on energy
               assimilation had the strongest effect on all life-history traits,
